Bingo! 🎯 The modulo operator (`%`) is exactly what we need to create that wrapping "donut" grid.

In JavaScript, if our width is `W = 10` and we check the right neighbor of a cell at index `9`, we do `(9 + 1) % 10`, which equals `0`. Perfect wrap!

There is just one tiny trap with modulo in JavaScript: it doesn't handle negative numbers the way we want for geometry. If we check the left neighbor of index `0`, `(0 - 1) % 10` gives `-1`, which will break our array.
To fix this, we simply add the width before we modulo: `(0 - 1 + 10) % 10 = 9`.

